#!/usr/bin/env python3
# Id: game_compat
# Display name: Game compatibility
# 显示名称: 游戏兼容性
# Description: Check a game's Linux/Proton compatibility and anti-cheat. Query a game's Linux compatibility from three sources: ProtonDB ratings and player reports, AreWeAntiCheatYet anti-cheat status, and the caniplayonlinux snapshot. Accepts a Steam App ID (number) or a game name. Content is parsed live from third-party sites; a missing source is not a negative verdict.
# Timeout: 60
# Group: gaming
# Trust: external
# Permission: read-only
# Example: {"query":"Elden Ring"}
# Parameters:
# {
#   "type": "object",
#   "properties": {
#     "query": {
#       "type": "string",
#       "description": "Steam App ID or game name."
#     },
#     "max_reports": {
#       "type": "integer",
#       "description": "Number of ProtonDB reports, default 8, max 40. Use 0 for the verdict only, without reports."
#     }
#   },
#   "required": [
#     "query"
#   ]
# }
"""
Linux game compatibility from three sources, merged into one Markdown answer.

Port of the built-in Rust tool (src/tools/protondb_query.rs + caniplayonlinux_query.rs
+ awacy_query.rs). Same pipeline:

  1. ProtonDB's Algolia index resolves the query (App ID or name, Chinese names
     included) into an English name + Steam App ID.
  2. ProtonDB summary + player reports, AreWeAntiCheatYet games.json and the
     caniplayonlinux page are fetched concurrently using that identity.
  3. Everything is rendered as Markdown; a source that has no entry is printed
     as "no result", a source that failed is printed as "error" -- never confused.

Usage (terminal):
    game_compat "Elden Ring"
    game_compat 1245620 --max-reports 3
    game_compat 黑神话悟空

Tool layer (Miyu) feeds a JSON object on stdin instead:
    {"query": "Apex Legends", "max_reports": 8}

Caches (24h, keyed by file mtime) live in $MIYU_SCRIPT_CACHE_DIR, falling back
to ~/.cache/miyu-scripts:
    awacy-games.json             full AreWeAntiCheatYet table (~460KB)
    caniplayonlinux-sitemap.xml  site map used for fuzzy slug matching
"""

